content.text:
I reviewed the whole target scope, since none of these files are committed yet. All eight angles were run in this context and duplicates were merged; findings were not verified, as the review spec asks. I found 10 issues, most severe first.

The only CLAUDE.md rules that apply are in `D:\video-indexing-yolo11\CLAUDE.md` (the user-level file only covers graphify). I found no clear violation I could quote against an exact line. The closest is the "requirements.txt (exact pins)" deliverable, which I folded into finding 4.

Two findings rest on Ultralytics behaviour I recalled rather than checked, because Ultralytics isn't installed in `.venv`: finding 2 (`YOLO_OFFLINE`) and finding 4 (Ultralytics pulling in `opencv-python`).

```json
[
  {
    "file": "run.py",
    "line": 82,
    "summary": "A GPU/inference failure is caught as if the video were broken: the video gets a 'could not decode video' error line and is marked done, so a broken CUDA context ruins every remaining video and --resume never retries them.",
    "failure_scenario": "A CUDA OOM or device-side assert happens in infer_fn while processing video 100. That video gets partial seconds plus an error line. The CUDA context stays broken, so videos 101..1600 each fail on their first batch and each gets a single error line. Every one is added to results.jsonl.done, so `--resume` skips them all. The run 'completes' with about 1500 bogus error lines and fails the accuracy check."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/model.py",
    "line": 46,
    "summary": "YOLO_OFFLINE is set to \"1\", but Ultralytics only switches to offline mode when the value is \"true\" (case-insensitive), so offline mode is never turned on.",
    "failure_scenario": "On the grader's machine with no internet, importing Ultralytics still runs is_online(), which tries socket connections to 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8 with 2 s timeouts. Any online-gated code (hub/asset checks) still tries to reach the network. The import is slower and the intended safeguard does nothing. Setting it to \"True\" fixes it."
  },
  {
    "file": "run.py",
    "line": 132,
    "summary": "dump_frames runs inside the same try as building the output lines, so if the optional diagnostic dump fails, the video's correct results are thrown away and replaced by a single error line.",
    "failure_scenario": "Run with `--dump-frames DIR` where DIR is on a full or read-only disk, or np.savez_compressed raises. The except branch sets `lines = [error_line(...)]`, which discards the per-second lines already computed. The video is written as an error and marked done, so its seconds become false negatives."
  },
  {
    "file": "requirements.txt",
    "line": 6,
    "summary": "Ultralytics declares `opencv-python` (not headless) as a dependency, so pip installs an unpinned opencv-python next to the pinned opencv-python-headless. Both install into the same cv2 package, and that cv2 version is not pinned.",
    "failure_scenario": "On a fresh VM, `pip install -r requirements.txt` installs both OpenCV wheels. Which cv2 you get depends on install order, and removing either one breaks cv2. Recent opencv-python releases require numpy>=2, which conflicts with numpy==1.26.4, so pip has to backtrack. The run environment is not reproducible from 'exact pins' (that is also why the README needs libgl1)."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/decode.py",
    "line": 44,
    "summary": "The Timestamper sets first_pts from the first frame that has a pts, not from the first decoded frame. If the leading frames have no pts, their estimated timestamps overlap the real ones and every later frame lands earlier than it should.",
    "failure_scenario": "At 30 fps, frames 0..29 have pts=None and get t=0..29/30. Frame 30 has a pts, becomes first_pts, and gets t=0. From then on every frame is stamped 1 s too early, so second N holds the frames of second N+1 and objects and people are reported one second off for the whole video."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/decode.py",
    "line": 59,
    "summary": "When no frame rate is known, a frame without pts gets t = index/30, an absolute guess that ignores the pts timeline of the frames around it.",
    "failure_scenario": "A 60 fps video with no guessed or average rate has frame 600 (real t≈10 s) with pts=None. It gets t=20 s and lands in second 20, which skews that second's half-frame vote and median. If it is near the end it can add trailing seconds the reference doesn't have, counted as extra seconds. It should use last_t plus the median gap between earlier timestamps."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/output.py",
    "line": 30,
    "summary": "With --resume, a missing sidecar (.done) file makes done empty, and the existing results file is then atomically rewritten as empty, silently wiping prior results.",
    "failure_scenario": "On Colab, only results.jsonl is copied or synced back, not results.jsonl.done, or the sidecar is deleted. Running `run.py --resume` then truncates results.jsonl to 0 bytes and redoes every video, losing hours of work with no warning. Resume should refuse, or rebuild done from complete videos, when the output exists but the sidecar does not."
  },
  {
    "file": "run.py",
    "line": 64,
    "summary": "process_frames keeps a full Record for every frame of the video (Fraction, two 5-element lists, pts, rotation, shape tuple), even though only (t, counts) is needed unless --dump-frames is set.",
    "failure_scenario": "A 1-hour 60 fps video has about 216k frames. At roughly 500-700 B per Record that is about 100-150 MB of Python objects held per video, along with GC pressure. Per-second aggregation could be streamed, or Records kept only when dump_frames is enabled."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/model.py",
    "line": 74,
    "summary": "Each result calls `.cpu()` twice (cls and conf), forcing two GPU-to-host syncs per frame, 64 per batch of 32.",
    "failure_scenario": "Because of these per-frame syncs, the step after inference serializes on many small transfers. One `r.boxes.data.cpu()` per result (or one transfer for the whole batch), then slicing columns 5 and 4, does the same work with a single transfer."
  },
  {
    "file": "tools/batch_agreement.py",
    "line": 49,
    "summary": "batch_agreement re-implements run.process_frames' batching and shape-flush rule by hand, instead of calling process_frames as tools/decoder_agreement.py does.",
    "failure_scenario": "If run.py's batching changes (pipelining, a new flush rule, sorting), this tool keeps validating the old hand-copied logic and reports agreement for code the pipeline no longer runs. Feeding Frame objects into run.process_frames removes the duplicated logic."
  }
]
```
